  "summary": "An airlift pump has no moving parts in the liquid. Compressed air is released through small holes near the bottom of a vertical riser pipe that stands in the liquid. The air–water mixture inside the riser is lighter than the water outside, so the outside water pressure (submergence) pushes the mixture up and out of the top. The deeper the injection point relative to the lift, the better the efficiency; typical submergence ratios are 50–70 %. Used for well development, aquaculture, wastewater sludge and grit transfer, and pumping corrosive or abrasive liquids.",
  "classification": {
    "family": "Kinetic / buoyancy (gas-lift)",
    "configuration": "Airlift riser with foot-piece air injector"
  },
  "model_configuration": {
    "submergence_ratio_note": "Submergence ratio = submergence / (submergence + lift); ~0.5–0.7 typical (verify for design)",
    "water_level_m": 2.0,
    "riser_diameter_mm": 100.0
  },
